\title{\bfseries Usurping Quantum Mechanics II\\[0.3em]
\large Tensor Filtration and the Paraconsistent Original}
\author{C. Lando Mills\\ \small Trabuco Canyon, California}
\date{3 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Quantum mechanics is the classical-logical image of a paraconsistent four-valued
logic. The ambient of the world is the paraconsistent trilattice of sixteen values,
and the consistent classical world is a restriction cut out of it by two retractions
that discard the states carrying contradiction and incompleteness. This paper carries
the mechanics of that reading and grounds it in a complete worked instance. It builds
the carrier explicitly, with its three orders, its negation, and its consequence
relation, and proves that the classical Boolean values are a proper part reached by
two distinct adjunctions, ex falso and ex quodlibet, and that the paraconsistent value
is inexpressible inside either classical image. It develops the operation at the
center of the account, tensor filtration, as an explicit ladder of raising, lowering,
and diagonal operators on the weight sectors of a many-coordinate register, and shows
that measurement is filtration through this ladder rather than any collapse. It then
folds in a full instance in which the classical image of a paraconsistent object is
computed and measured. The problem of Optimal Polynomial Intersection has a quantum
algorithm, Decoded Quantum Interferometry, whose expected performance is the largest
eigenvalue of a finite tridiagonal matrix. We show that this matrix is the ladder of a
paraconsistent token-word, the word's split, fuse, and evaluate marks realized as
those very operators, and that the algorithm's expectation value is that token-word
read through the coreflector. We compute the finite operator-valued value and its
asymptotic scalar limit and measure, across four scales, the richer finite description
collapsing onto the poorer limit in the exact direction the coreflector moves. The
dimensionless constants are treated elsewhere; the spine here is the filtration
mechanics and the worked convergence, all proved or computed on the page.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

The physics of the last century is usually told as a story of a classical world
disturbed. There is a definite world of things with definite properties, and quantum
mechanics is the strange correction that appears on close inspection: particles in two
places, cats alive and dead, measurements that create the reality they record. The
classical world is the ground and the quantum is the anomaly laid over it.

This program inverts that story and shows the inversion is forced. The ground is not
classical. The ambient logic of the world is paraconsistent, a four-valued logic in
which a proposition can carry support for its truth and support for its falsity at once
without the whole system exploding into triviality. The consistent two-valued world is
not the floor beneath the quantum; it is a restriction cut out of the paraconsistent
ambient after the fact, a proper part reachable from the whole by a retraction that
throws information away. Quantum mechanics is the ambient itself, read through the
classical slice we impose when we insist every proposition be true or false and
nothing else. The world does not appear classical at any scale; the classical
description is a bookkeeping laid over the ambient that declines to look at the
structure constituting it.

This paper carries two things the setting of the direction did not. First, it develops
the mechanics of the operation at the center of the account. Measurement is not a
collapse; it is a tensor filtration, a reversible selection of one factor of a tensor
while the others remain. We make the filtration concrete as an explicit ladder of
operators on the weight sectors of a many-coordinate register: a raising operator that
opens a value into more support, its adjoint the lowering operator, and a diagonal
operator that reads support already present. The three are the split, the fuse, and
the evaluate marks of the grammar, realized as linear operators, and together they are
a tridiagonal matrix whose structure is the whole content of the filtration.

Second, it folds in a complete worked instance in which the classical image of a
paraconsistent object is computed and measured. The problem is Optimal Polynomial
Intersection, a hard combinatorial optimization over a finite field. Its
state-of-the-art quantum algorithm, Decoded Quantum Interferometry, has an expected
performance governed by the largest eigenvalue of a finite tridiagonal matrix. We show
that this matrix is the ladder of a paraconsistent token-word, the classical image of
that word, and that the algorithm's expectation value is the word read through the
coreflector. We compute the finite operator-valued value and its asymptotic scalar
limit and measure the one collapsing onto the other across four scales, the richer
description giving way to the poorer in the direction the coreflector moves.

A word of terminology. Throughout we call a string of the twelve grammar marks a
\emph{token-word}, to keep it distinct from a word in the ordinary sense. A token-word
is a program on the carrier, and the operations it is built from are the marks named in
Section 2. The dimensionless constants of physics are treated in the companion part of
this program and are not discussed here. The spine of this paper is the filtration
mechanics and the worked convergence, and everything is proved or computed on the
page rather than by reference.

\section{The four-valued carrier}

\subsection{Two layers, four base values, sixteen states}

A four-valued logic asks not whether a proposition is true or false but what
information is present about it, separating support for truth from support for falsity.
Two independent bits carry the constructive layer: $T$, that support for truth is
present, and $F$, that support for falsity is present. Both absent gives the
undetermined value $N$, both present the overdetermined value $B$, and these four,
$\{N,T,F,B\}$, are the Belnap four. The trilattice of Shramko, Dunn and
Takenaka~\cite{sdt} adds a second, informational layer, an acceptable value $t$ and a
rejectable value $f$ that carry weight without settling truth. A value of the full
carrier is any subset of the four base marks $\{T,F,t,f\}$, and there are sixteen.

\begin{definition}[The carrier]
Let $\mathbf{16} = \mathcal{P}(\{T,F,t,f\})$, equivalently $\{0,1\}^4$, with bottom
$N = \{\}$ and top $A = \{T,F,t,f\}$. The constructive layer of a value $x$ is
$x \cap \{T,F\}$ and its informational layer is $x \cap \{t,f\}$.
\end{definition}

The Belnap four is the part with empty informational layer: $N$, $T=\{T\}$, $F=\{F\}$,
$B=\{T,F\}$. The classical values are the two one-sided among these, $T$ and $F$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{l l c l l c}
\toprule
value & marks & info & value & marks & info \\
\midrule
$N$    & $\{\}$      & 0 & $tf$   & $\{t,f\}$     & 2 \\
$T$    & $\{T\}$     & 1 & $Ttf$  & $\{T,t,f\}$   & 3 \\
$F$    & $\{F\}$     & 1 & $Ftf$  & $\{F,t,f\}$   & 3 \\
$t$    & $\{t\}$     & 1 & $TFt$  & $\{T,F,t\}$   & 3 \\
$f$    & $\{f\}$     & 1 & $TFf$  & $\{T,F,f\}$   & 3 \\
$B$    & $\{T,F\}$   & 2 & $Tt$   & $\{T,t\}$     & 2 \\
$Tf$   & $\{T,f\}$   & 2 & $Ff$   & $\{F,f\}$     & 2 \\
$Ft$   & $\{F,t\}$   & 2 & $A$    & $\{T,F,t,f\}$ & 4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Three orders, three lattices}

\begin{definition}[The three orders]
For $x,y \in \mathbf{16}$: the information order $x \leqi y$ is $x \subseteq y$; the
truth order $x \leqt y$ holds when $x \cap \{T,t\} \subseteq y \cap \{T,t\}$ and
$y \cap \{F,f\} \subseteq x \cap \{F,f\}$; the constructivity order $x \leqc y$ holds
when $x \cap \{T,F\} \subseteq y \cap \{T,F\}$ and $y \cap \{t,f\} \subseteq x \cap \{t,f\}$.
\end{definition}

The information order asks how much is known, and moving up it only adds marks. The
truth order asks how true a value is, truth support growing and falsity support
shrinking. The constructivity order asks how settled a value is, the constructive layer
growing and the informational layer shrinking.

\begin{proposition}[Partial orders and lattices]\label{prop:orders}
Each of $\leqi$, $\leqt$, $\leqc$ is a partial order, and each is a lattice: the
$\leqi$ join and meet are union and intersection; the $\leqt$ join takes the truth
marks by union and the falsity marks by intersection, and its meet the reverse; the
$\leqc$ join and meet are the same with the two halves $\{T,F\}$ and $\{t,f\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $\leqi$ this is the powerset order. For $\leqt$, write $u(x) = x\cap\{T,t\}$ and
$v(x) = x\cap\{F,f\}$, so $x\leqt y$ is $u(x)\subseteq u(y)$ and $v(y)\subseteq v(x)$;
reflexivity is immediate, transitivity follows from transitivity of $\subseteq$ in each
half, and antisymmetry holds because $\{T,t\}$ and $\{F,f\}$ partition the marks so a
value is fixed by its two halves. An upper bound $z$ of $x,y$ needs
$u(z)\supseteq u(x)\cup u(y)$ and $v(z)\subseteq v(x)\cap v(y)$, and the value with
equality in both is below every other upper bound, the join; the meet is dual. For
$\leqc$ the argument is identical with the halves $\{T,F\}$ and $\{t,f\}$.
\end{proof}

On the Belnap four the truth order is $F \leqt N \leqt T$ and $F \leqt B \leqt T$ with
$N,B$ incomparable, so $F$ is the bottom and $T$ the top. The truth-order meet and join
on the Belnap four are

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|cccc}
$\wedge_{\mathrm t}$ & $N$ & $T$ & $F$ & $B$ \\
\hline
$N$ & $N$ & $N$ & $F$ & $F$ \\
$T$ & $N$ & $T$ & $F$ & $B$ \\
$F$ & $F$ & $F$ & $F$ & $F$ \\
$B$ & $F$ & $B$ & $F$ & $B$ \\
\end{tabular}
\qquad\qquad
\begin{tabular}{c|cccc}
$\vee_{\mathrm t}$ & $N$ & $T$ & $F$ & $B$ \\
\hline
$N$ & $N$ & $T$ & $N$ & $T$ \\
$T$ & $T$ & $T$ & $T$ & $T$ \\
$F$ & $N$ & $T$ & $F$ & $B$ \\
$B$ & $T$ & $T$ & $B$ & $B$ \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

so $B \wedge_{\mathrm t} \neg B = B \wedge_{\mathrm t} B = B \neq F$ and
$B \vee_{\mathrm t} \neg B = B \neq T$: a contradiction met or joined with its own
negation is the contradiction, held rather than forced to a pole.

\subsection{Negation, engage, and the twelve marks}

\begin{definition}[Negation and engage]
Negation $\neg$ exchanges $T$ with $F$ and $t$ with $f$; in bits
$\neg(a,b,c,d)=(b,a,d,c)$. Engage $\mengagr$ sets both informational marks,
$\mengagr(a,b,c,d)=(a,b,1,1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Two fixed points]\label{prop:neg}
$\neg$ is an involution reversing $\leqt$ and preserving $\leqi$, with fixed points
exactly $N$ and $B$ on the Belnap four, while $\neg T = F$ and $\neg F = T$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Applying the swap twice restores each bit. Negation exchanges the two halves of the
$\leqt$ condition, reversing that order, and permutes marks by presence, preserving
$\subseteq$. Fixed points: $\neg\{\}=\{\}$ and $\neg\{T,F\}=\{F,T\}=\{T,F\}$, while
$\neg\{T\}=\{F\}\neq\{T\}$.
\end{proof}

The two negation-stable values are the two nonclassical ones. Negation moves the
consistent values and cannot move the undetermined or the overdetermined, the first
sign of the asymmetry the paper turns on.

The operations on the carrier are drawn from a fixed alphabet of twelve marks, and we
name them once, since the filtration and the worked instance use them as operators. A
token-word is a string over these twelve.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c l l}
\toprule
mark & name & operation \\
\midrule
$\mvinit$ & begin & open the token-word; the source boundary \\
$\mtanch$ & close & close the token-word; the sink boundary \\
$\mafwd$ & forward & the forward morphism, a step up in magnitude or scale \\
$\marev$ & reverse & the reverse morphism, the negation involution's carrier \\
$\mclink$ & compose & chain two operations; accumulate along a path \\
$\mimscrib$ & self-reference & the identity that refers a value to itself \\
$\msplit$ & split & the one brancher; open a value into its arms \\
$\mfuse$ & fuse & the one merger; close arms back into a value \\
$\mevalt$ & evaluate true & read the truth arm; deposit truth support \\
$\mevalf$ & evaluate false & read the falsity arm; deposit falsity support \\
$\mengagr$ & engage & hold both; set the informational layer \\
$\mifix$ & fix & the irreversible commit; the winding record \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Three marks move the structural verdict of a token-word: the split $\msplit$ opens a
fork, the fuse $\mfuse$ closes one, and the close $\mtanch$ anchors the token-word. The
split and fuse are the operations whose composite is the identity, and whose realization
as ladder operators is the mechanics of Section 5.

\subsection{Entailment without explosion}

The logic is called First-Degree Entailment because its consequence relation keeps
contradictions local. For token-words built from the connectives $\wedge_{\mathrm t}$,
$\vee_{\mathrm t}$, $\neg$, write $A \models B$ when for every assignment of carrier
values to the atoms, $\llbracket A \rrbracket \leqt \llbracket B \rrbracket$: the value
of $A$ is at least as true as $B$ under every valuation.

\begin{theorem}[No explosion, no excluded middle]\label{thm:entail}
A contradiction does not entail an arbitrary conclusion, and a formula joined with its
negation is not a tautology. There are assignments with
$A \wedge_{\mathrm t} \neg A \not\models C$ and with
$\llbracket A \vee_{\mathrm t} \neg A \rrbracket \neq T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the first, take atoms $A,C$ and the assignment $A=B$, $C=F$; then
$\llbracket A \wedge_{\mathrm t}\neg A\rrbracket = B\wedge_{\mathrm t}B = B$ while
$\llbracket C\rrbracket = F$, and $B\not\leqt F$ since $F$ is the truth-order bottom.
For the second, take $A=N$; then $N\vee_{\mathrm t}\neg N = N \neq T$, so
$A\vee_{\mathrm t}\neg A$ is not a tautology.
\end{proof}

The value $B$ blocks explosion, taking a value above the truth-order bottom so a
contradiction does not lie below every conclusion; the value $N$ blocks excluded
middle. Removing both recovers classical consequence, and that removal is the
restriction of the next section.

\section{The classical restriction}

The passage to a consistent world is a retraction onto the Boolean core, lossy in a
precise sense we prove, because the worked instance measures exactly this loss.

\begin{definition}[Boolean core and the two collapses]
Let $\mathbf{B}=\{F,T\}$ with inherited truth order and negation, the two-element
Boolean algebra, exactly the Belnap values with $x\vee_{\mathrm t}\neg x = T$. Let the
ex falso map $c$ and ex quodlibet map $r$ send $\{N,T,F,B\}$ to $\mathbf{B}$ by
$c(T)=T,c(F)=F,c(N)=F,c(B)=F$ and $r(T)=T,r(F)=F,r(N)=T,r(B)=T$, and let $\iota$ be the
inclusion.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two distinct adjunctions]\label{thm:two}
$c$ is monotone, fixes $\mathbf{B}$, and is right adjoint to $\iota$ in the truth
order, $\iota(x)\leqt v \iff x\leqt c(v)$. $r$ is monotone, fixes $\mathbf{B}$, and is
left adjoint, $r(v)\leqt x \iff v\leqt\iota(x)$. They differ on the nonclassical
values, $r(N)=r(B)=T\neq F=c(N)=c(B)$, and agree on $\mathbf{B}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The truth-order covers are $F\leqt N$, $F\leqt B$, $N\leqt T$, $B\leqt T$. Under $c$
they map to $F\leqt F,F\leqt F,F\leqt T,F\leqt T$, all true, so $c$ is monotone. The
adjunction, two cases: for $x=F$ both $x\leqt c(v)$ and $\iota(x)\leqt v$ hold for all
$v$ since $F$ is the bottom; for $x=T$, $x\leqt c(v)$ forces $c(v)=T$, holding exactly
at $v=T$, and $\iota(T)\leqt v$ forces $v=T$. Under $r$ the covers map to
$F\leqt T,F\leqt T,T\leqt T,T\leqt T$, all true; for $x=T$ both sides hold for all $v$,
and for $x=F$, $r(v)\leqt F$ forces $r(v)=F$ at exactly $v=F$, while $v\leqt\iota(F)=F$
forces $v=F$. Distinctness is direct.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Neither is an information-order adjunction]\label{prop:noinfo}
In the information order, where $N$ is the bottom and $B$ the top, neither $c$ nor $r$
is adjoint to $\iota$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Were $r$ left adjoint in $\leqi$, then $v=B$, $x=T$ would need
$r(B)\leqi T \iff B\leqi\iota(T)=T$; the right side is false ($B$ is the top), the left
side $T\leqi T$ true. Were $c$ right adjoint, $v=N$, $x=F$ would need
$\iota(F)=F\leqi N \iff F\leqi c(N)=F$; the left side is false ($N$ is the bottom), the
right true.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The collapse is exactly lossy]\label{thm:lossy}
Let $\mathrm{inc}$ send every Belnap value to $B$. It is idempotent with unique fixed
point $B$, and there is no map $\varphi:\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{B}$ with
$\iota\circ\varphi = \mathrm{inc}\circ\iota$: the value $B$ is inexpressible inside
either classical image.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\mathrm{inc}(\mathrm{inc}(x))=\mathrm{inc}(B)=B=\mathrm{inc}(x)$, and a fixed point
solves $x=B$. If $\varphi$ existed, $\iota(\varphi(x))=\mathrm{inc}(\iota(x))=B$ for
classical $x$; but $\iota$ has image $\{F,T\}$, which excludes $B$, so no value of
$\varphi(x)$ maps to $B$.
\end{proof}

The two retractions each give a total map onto the core, so no value is sent nowhere;
what fails is expression, the operation whose fixed point is the contradiction cannot
be written inside the image. The three-valued restriction removing only $B$, keeping
$\{N,T,F\}$, is again a truth-order coreflection, by the same two-case argument, so
removing contradiction alone and removing both contradiction and incompleteness are
the same kind of construction at two depths. Passing to a consistent world is not a
change of coordinates a richer bookkeeping could undo; it removes a value the remaining
structure has no name for. The worked instance measures how much a specific
paraconsistent object exceeds its classical retraction, and finds the excess positive
at every finite size and shrinking toward the retraction as the size grows.

\section{Tensor filtration}

The operation at the center of the account is the split and its adjoint fuse, and their
action on a many-coordinate register is a ladder of operators we build explicitly. This
is the mechanics that measurement, and the worked instance, run on.

\subsection{The register and its weight sectors}

Take $m$ copies of the constructive layer, an $m$-coordinate register in which each
coordinate carries a one-sided value $T$ or $F$. A basis state is a string
$x\in\{T,F\}^m$, its weight the number of coordinates at $T$, and the weight-$k$ sector
the $\binom{m}{k}$ strings of weight $k$.

\begin{definition}[Uniform sector states]
For $0\leq k\leq m$ let
$\ket{k} = \binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\sum_{\mathrm{wt}(x)=k}\ket{x}$, the uniform normalized
superposition of the weight-$k$ strings. The $\ket{k}$ are orthonormal and span the
symmetric subspace, the part invariant under permuting coordinates.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Split as raising, fuse as lowering}

\begin{theorem}[The ladder operators]\label{thm:ladder}
Let $S_{+}$ send a weight-$k$ string to the sum of its weight-$(k+1)$ extensions, each
raising one coordinate from $F$ to $T$, and let $S_{-}$ be its adjoint. Then
\[
S_{+}\ket{k} = \sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\,\ket{k+1},
\qquad
S_{-}\ket{k} = \sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\,\ket{k-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Acting with $S_{+}$, each weight-$k$ string $x$ maps to the sum of the $m-k$ strings
obtained by raising one of its $F$ coordinates, so
$S_{+}\ket{k} = \binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\sum_{\mathrm{wt}(x)=k}\sum_{i:x_i=F}\ket{x^{(i)}}$.
Each weight-$(k+1)$ string $y$ arises once for each of its $k+1$ coordinates at $T$,
since lowering any one gives a weight-$k$ string that raises back to $y$, so the
coefficient of $\ket{y}$ is $(k+1)\binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}$, and
$S_{+}\ket{k} = (k+1)\binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\binom{m}{k+1}^{1/2}\ket{k+1}$. With
$\binom{m}{k+1}/\binom{m}{k} = (m-k)/(k+1)$ the coefficient is $\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$. The
adjoint gives $\langle k-1\mid S_{-}\mid k\rangle = \langle k\mid S_{+}\mid k-1\rangle
= \sqrt{k(m-k+1)}$, so $S_{-}\ket{k}=\sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\ket{k-1}$.
\end{proof}

The split $\msplit$ is $S_{+}$, opening one coordinate into truth support; the fuse
$\mfuse$ is $S_{-}$, its adjoint, closing one back. Their composite returns the sector
up to the ladder coefficients, the operator form of the counit-unit law that opening
and closing lose nothing.

\subsection{Evaluate as the diagonal, and the tridiagonal ladder}

\begin{proposition}[The diagonal operator]\label{prop:diag}
For a fixed real $d$ let $D$ read $d$ once per coordinate at $T$. Then
$D\ket{k}=kd\,\ket{k}$, diagonal in the sector basis.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
On a weight-$k$ string $D$ reads $d$ exactly $k$ times, so $D\ket{x}=kd\ket{x}$, and
every string in $\ket{k}$ has weight $k$, so $D\ket{k}=kd\ket{k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The filtration matrix]\label{thm:matrix}
On the span of $\ket{0},\dots,\ket{\ell}$ the operator $A = D + S_{+} + S_{-}$ is the
$(\ell+1)\times(\ell+1)$ real symmetric tridiagonal matrix with $A_{kk}=kd$ and
$A_{k,k+1}=A_{k+1,k}=\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The diagonal is $D$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:diag}. The first off-diagonals are the
raising and lowering matrix elements of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}, equal, so symmetric.
All other entries vanish because $S_{\pm}$ change the weight by one and $D$ preserves
it.
\end{proof}

A small case makes the ladder concrete. At $\ell=2$ the matrix is
\[
A = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & \sqrt{m} & 0 \\
\sqrt{m} & d & \sqrt{2(m-1)} \\
0 & \sqrt{2(m-1)} & 2d
\end{pmatrix},
\]
the diagonal counting truth support at $0,d,2d$, and the off-diagonals the
raising-lowering coefficients $\sqrt{m}$ and $\sqrt{2(m-1)}$. This is the whole content
of the filtration on three sectors: the split and fuse move between adjacent weights,
the evaluate reads the weight in place, and nothing else acts.

\subsection{Reversibility and the Frobenius pair}

The split and fuse are a comultiplication and a multiplication. The split $\delta$
opens a value into a deeper register where its arms are separate; the fuse $\mu$ closes
them back. Their sharpest law is the counit-unit identity, that fuse after split is the
identity on the shallow side, and the Frobenius compatibility that lets the fuse slide
across the split,
\[
(\mu\otimes\mathrm{id})\circ(\mathrm{id}\otimes\delta)
= \delta\circ\mu
= (\mathrm{id}\otimes\mu)\circ(\delta\otimes\mathrm{id}).
\]
For the mechanics we need two consequences, both proved above in the ladder: the round
trip fuse-after-split loses nothing, since $S_-$ is the adjoint of $S_+$ and their
composite is diagonal and invertible on the interior sectors; and the operator
$A = D + S_+ + S_-$ is self-adjoint, so it generates a reversible motion on the ladder.
A Frobenius pair with these properties is a reversible open-and-close: the opening is
undone by the closing.

\subsection{Measurement is filtration through the ladder}

Measurement selects a sector. The split opens the register up the ladder, a chosen
sector is read, and the fuse closes it back, and because the fuse is the adjoint of the
split the whole ladder persists through the reading. What a classical account records as
a collapse to a definite weight is the reading of one rung while the ladder remains, and
the rungs not read are the tensor factors the split opened and the fuse can restore.
There is no collapse in the ladder; there is a filtration that selects a rung and a fuse
that recovers the whole. The apparent destruction of the other rungs is the classical
slice's inability to carry more than the one it read, the inexpressibility of
Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy} at the level of the register.

The word tensor is exact. The split opens a value into a pair of tensor factors; a
reading selects one factor while the other remains a factor of the whole; the fuse
recombines them. Reading $x\otimes y$ reads $x$ without deleting $y$. A genuine
collapse, an actual destruction of the unread factor, would be an erasure with a
Landauer cost~\cite{landauer}, and by the reversible-computing models of
Bennett~\cite{bennett} and of Fredkin and Toffoli~\cite{fredkin} a computation built
from reversible operations owes no such cost. The filtration erases nothing, the fuse
restores the factor, and the ambient pays no thermodynamic price for measurement; the
price appears only in the classical image, which keeps one factor and drops the other.

\section{The phenomena}

We place the phenomena of quantum mechanics on the carrier and the ladder, each the
classical image of one feature.

\subsection{Superposition is the value both}

A qubit in superposition is the value $B$, true and false held together, its own
negation, the one Belnap value the classical slice cannot name. The Hadamard gate sends
each classical value to $B$ and $B$ back to a definite value; that a definite input
yields the held value is $\mengagr$ at the constructive layer. On the register a
superposition is a state spread across weight sectors, the uniform sector states its
symmetric instances.

\subsection{The Born rule is a forced quadratic projection}

The probability of an outcome is the overlap of the state with one pole. The pure
states of a qubit are the rays of a two-dimensional complex space, a sphere with the
true pole $T$ at the north, $\theta=0$, the false pole $F$ at the south, $\theta=\pi$,
and the balanced held value $B$ on the equator. A pure state at polar angle $\theta$ is
the ray of $\ket{\psi}=\cos(\theta/2)\ket{T}+e^{i\varphi}\sin(\theta/2)\ket{F}$, the
half-angle arising because the sphere is the projective line, a full turn on the sphere
being a half turn in the ray. The truth measurement is the pair of antipodal projectors,
and
\[
p_T(\theta) = \bigl|\langle T\mid\psi\rangle\bigr|^2 = \cos^2\tfrac{\theta}{2}
= \frac{1+\cos\theta}{2}, \qquad p_F = \sin^2\tfrac{\theta}{2}, \qquad p_T+p_F=1,
\]
giving $p_T=1$ at the true pole, $0$ at the false pole, and $1/2$ at the balanced value
on the equator, the last recovering the cardinality reading
$p_T = \mathrm{info}(\psi\cap\{T\})/\mathrm{info}(\psi)$ as the equatorial case. The
quadratic form is forced, not chosen: a probability assignment on a positive-operator
frame that is additive and non-contextual has the form $\mathrm{tr}(\rho E)$ by Busch's
theorem~\cite{busch}, extending Gleason's~\cite{gleason} to every dimension including
two, and on the truth projector this is $\cos^2(\theta/2)$. On the full carrier the sum
$p_T+p_F$ falls below one exactly by the informational-layer weight the truth
measurement does not reach.

\subsection{Interference is a winding on a lattice of turns}

The phase $e^{i\varphi}$ that produces interference is a winding, a rational number of
turns, fixed at the mark $\mifix$. The native windings sit on a lattice of tenths.

\begin{proposition}[The gate lattice]\label{prop:tenths}
$\tfrac12=\tfrac{5}{10}$ lies on the tenths lattice, and $\tfrac18$ and $\tfrac14$ do
not: $\tfrac18=\tfrac{p}{10}$ forces $p=5/4$ and $\tfrac14=\tfrac{p}{10}$ forces
$p=5/2$, neither an integer.
\end{proposition}

Interference between two paths is the difference of their windings; a half turn is on
the lattice and exact, the eighth turn of the gate usually written $T$ is off it, which
is why gate compilation exists. The fringe visibility is the information-order distance
between the two path states.

\subsection{Entanglement is a persistent contradiction}

Two systems are entangled when a shared held value $B$ persists as each side is read.
Because $B$ survives refutation, is fixed by negation, and does not trivialize the
logic, the correlation exceeds any assignment of independent definite values. Its
strength is a single tilt angle. Taking the four settings~\cite{bell,chsh}
$a=0,a'=\pi/2,b=\pi/4,b'=3\pi/4$ with correlator $E(x,y)=\cos(x-y)$,
\[
E(a,b)=\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\; E(a,b')=-\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\; E(a',b)=\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\;
E(a',b')=\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2},
\]
and $S=|E(a,b)-E(a,b')+E(a',b)+E(a',b')| = 4\cdot\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}=2\sqrt2$, the
Tsirelson bound~\cite{tsirelson}, the shared value supplying $\cos(\pi/4)$ per setting.

\subsection{Uncertainty and error correction are the composition rule}

A value produced inside a region and cleared by a reversal is lost unless an enclosing
region banks a copy. Two quantities opened in regions that cannot nest cannot both be
banked, so one is exposed when the other closes.

\begin{lemma}[Banking survives a reversal]\label{lem:bank}
A mark deposited, then held by a frame open across a reversal, then fused, is present
after the fuse; a mark deposited with no enclosing frame open, then cleared by a
reversal, is lost and no later fuse restores it.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A deposit into an open frame is banked there; the reversal clears the register but not
the open frame, and the fuse folds the frame's copy back by mark-wise union, restoring
it. A deposit with no open frame is in the register only; the reversal removes it and no
frame holds a copy.
\end{proof}

Uncertainty is the exposed case forced by geometry: position and momentum are two
counts whose holding regions cannot both enclose the other's computing region, so one is
always exposed, giving $\Delta x\,\Delta p \geq c\hbar$ with $c$ a positive order-one
constant the rule fixes without evaluating, and $\hbar$ the readout unit supplied from
outside as $\pi$ is. Error correction is the banked case arranged deliberately: the
syndrome banked by an outer frame that stays open across the reversal clearing the
working register, restored by the outer fuse, correcting the error without disturbing
the encoded value. The same rule, obeyed, is error correction; forced to be violated,
it is uncertainty.

\subsection{Decoherence, complementarity, and the eraser}

Decoherence empties the informational layer $\{t,f\}$ to the environment, the
off-diagonal coherences of the density matrix carried off while the populations, the
diagonal read by the sector projection, remain; the truth fraction rises toward one and
the mixed state settles onto its constructive shadow. Complementarity is the two swaps
that factor negation, the truth swap and the information swap acting on disjoint pairs
of marks, so sharpening the constructive layer says nothing about the informational
layer the conjugate measurement reads. The quantum eraser is the fuse: marking a path is
a split that banks the which-path value, the interference absent while the frame is
open, and erasing the mark is the fuse that closes the frame, restoring the held value
and the interference with no reach back in time, the round trip being the identity.

\subsection{Contextuality is the frame-dependence of the held value}

\begin{proposition}[No global classical assignment]\label{prop:context}
There is no assignment of a single classical value to a proposition in the state $B$
that agrees with every frame, since the truth frame filters $B$ to $T$ and the falsity
frame filters $B$ to $F$, and $T\neq F$.
\end{proposition}

The impossibility is the inexpressibility of $B$ seen from the frames: the held value
has no classical name, so its reading is whatever the frame filters it to, and different
frames filter it differently. The value is one; its shadows are many, one per frame.

\subsection{Composite systems}

Two systems combine by the product of their carriers, and an entangled state is a value
of the product that does not factor into a value on each part. A shared held relation,
one part true and the other false held together as $B$ rather than settled, is not any
separable pair of one-sided values, since a pair names one alternative and the shared
value holds both. The four Bell states are the four shared held values of the two-part
product under the two swaps, and reading one part is a split of the shared value that,
by the persistence of $B$, does not destroy it, which is why the correlation holds
however the parts are read. The middle-value count multiplies under the product, so a
composite of many parts climbs quickly, the carrier's form of the exponential growth of
the state space with the number of parts.

\section{A worked realization: Optimal Polynomial Intersection}

We now carry a complete instance in which the classical image of a paraconsistent object
is computed and its convergence to the paraconsistent original measured. The object is
the tridiagonal ladder of Section 5, and the setting is a hard optimization whose
quantum algorithm that ladder governs.

\subsection{The problem}

\begin{definition}[Optimal Polynomial Intersection]
Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $n<p-1$. Let $F_1,\dots,F_{p-1}$ be subsets of
$\mathbb{F}_p$. An instance asks for a polynomial $Q\in\mathbb{F}_p[y]$ of degree at
most $n-1$ maximizing
$f_{\mathrm{OPI}}(Q) = |\{y\in\{1,\dots,p-1\}: Q(y)\in F_y\}|$. It is balanced when
every $|F_i|=r=\lfloor p/2\rfloor$.
\end{definition}

Optimal Polynomial Intersection is a maximum-satisfaction problem over $\mathbb{F}_p$:
a linear code fixes the bounded-degree polynomials as codewords, and maximizing the
intersection count is maximizing the number of satisfied constraints. The constraint
count is $m=p-1$, the constraint matrix is a Vandermonde matrix, and the dual code
$C^{\perp}$ is a Reed-Solomon code. Syndrome decoding of $C^{\perp}$ out to
$\ell=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor$ errors is the Berlekamp-Massey algorithm and runs in
polynomial time. The problem is a natural hard target because the optimum is not
reachable by the decoder alone; the quantum algorithm concentrates amplitude on
low-weight error patterns to do better than the classical baseline.

\subsection{The algorithm and its matrix}

Decoded Quantum Interferometry~\cite{dqi} prepares a state whose amplitude is
concentrated on low-weight error patterns and reads the decoded polynomial. Its
expected performance is the largest eigenvalue of a finite matrix.

\begin{lemma}[The expected satisfied count]\label{lem:jordan}
Let $\langle s^{(m,\ell)}\rangle$ be the expected number of satisfied constraints after
state preparation at decoding radius $\ell$. If $2\ell+1<d^{\perp}$, the minimum
distance of $C^{\perp}$, then
\[
\langle s^{(m,\ell)}\rangle = \frac{mr}{p} + \frac{\sqrt{r(p-r)}}{p}\,
\lambda_{\max}\bigl(A^{(m,\ell,d)}\bigr),
\]
with $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ the $(\ell+1)\times(\ell+1)$ real symmetric tridiagonal matrix,
diagonal $kd$ for $k=0,\dots,\ell$, off-diagonal $a_{k+1}=\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$, and
$d=(p-2r)/\sqrt{r(p-r)}$.
\end{lemma}

This is the result of Jordan and colleagues~\cite{dqi}, and its matrix is exactly the
filtration ladder of Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix}: the diagonal $kd$ is the evaluate
operator $D$ at $d=(p-2r)/\sqrt{r(p-r)}$, and the off-diagonal $\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$ is the
raising-lowering pair of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}. The expected satisfied count is a
constant plus the scaled largest eigenvalue of the ladder.

\begin{proposition}[The asymptotic limit]\label{prop:asymp}
Taking $\ell\to n/2$ and $m\to p$ gives
\[
\frac{\langle s\rangle_{\mathrm{DQI}}}{p} = \frac12 + \sqrt{\frac{n}{2p}\Bigl(1-\frac{n}{2p}\Bigr)},
\]
with no eigenvalue problem to solve.
\end{proposition}

The finite form keeps the full $(\ell+1)$-dimensional operator, an eigenvector, a
matrix, a specific finite $m$; the asymptotic form is what survives once that structure
is smoothed into a scalar as $m\to p$. The finite form carries strictly more than the
scalar at any finite $m$, and the direction from the one to the other is a coarsening,
the direction the coreflector of Theorem~\ref{thm:two} moves.

\subsection{The paraconsistent original}

We claim the matrix $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ is the classical image of a paraconsistent
token-word, and the algorithm's expectation value is that token-word read through the
coreflector. We exhibit the token-word, the lift, and the identification.

The syndrome-decoding structure of the algorithm, an outer frame banking a count across
an uncomputation step, is representable as a closing token-word over the twelve marks on
the Belnap slice. An auto-designed token-word for this structure, of thirty-three
marks, failed to close: audited by the closure instruments it exposed a count cleared
before the frame that would bank it was opened, the composition-rule violation of
Lemma~\ref{lem:bank} in which a result is exposed rather than banked. The diagnostic,
read at every rotation of the token-word's orbit, stated the same defect in the same
place: a count cleared by a reversal before the frame that would hold it has opened.
Applying the composition rule as a construction, opening the holding frame before the
computing frame, the repair adds five marks, three splits and two fuses, and the
thirty-eight-mark token-word closes. Its banked count survives every reversal, its
cyclic verdict is closing at every rotation of its orbit, and an exhaustive audit across
all rotations finds it holding where the original exposed weight at every cut. This is a
constructed, mechanically verified instance of a paraconsistent token-word of the class
the algorithm's circuit is the classical image of.

\begin{proposition}[The $m$-fold lift]\label{prop:lift}
Let $\iota$ and the coreflector $c$ act coordinatewise on $\{N,T,F,B\}^m$, ordered
coordinatewise in the truth order. Then the coordinatewise inclusion is left adjoint to
the coordinatewise coreflector, and the Boolean image $\mathbf{B}^m\cong\{F,T\}^m$ is
the algorithm's classical register $\mathbb{F}_2^m$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $u\in\mathbf{B}^m$ and $v\in\{N,T,F,B\}^m$, the coordinatewise order gives
$\iota^m(u)\leqt v$ iff $\iota(u_i)\leqt v_i$ for all $i$, and $u\leqt c^m(v)$ iff
$u_i\leqt c(v_i)$ for all $i$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:two} each coordinate satisfies
$\iota(u_i)\leqt v_i \iff u_i\leqt c(v_i)$, so the two coincide. The Boolean image
$\{F,T\}^m$ is $\mathbb{F}_2^m$ under $F\mapsto0$, $T\mapsto1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The matrix is the coreflector image of the token-word's ladder]\label{thm:main}
On the $m$-fold paraconsistent register, with $\ket{k}$ the uniform superposition of
states with $k$ coordinates at $T$, the split mark $\msplit$ is the raising operator
$S_{+}$, the fuse mark $\mfuse$ its adjoint $S_{-}$, and the evaluate mark $\mevalt$ the
diagonal $D$ reading $d$ once per coordinate at $T$. Then
$S_{-}\ket{k}=\sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\ket{k-1}$ and $D\ket{k}=kd\ket{k}$ are the off-diagonal
$a_k$ and the diagonal $kd$ of $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ in Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan}, term for
term, at $d=(p-2r)/\sqrt{r(p-r)}$. Read through the coreflector of
Theorem~\ref{thm:two}, $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ is the effective operator governing the
algorithm's expectation value $\langle s^{(m,\ell)}\rangle$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The three marks realize as the three operators by the definitions of Section 5: opening
a frame raises the weight, closing lowers it, depositing reads the present weight. Their
matrix elements on the uniform sector states are those of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder} and
Proposition~\ref{prop:diag}, which are the entries of $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ in
Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan}. The coreflector $c^m$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:lift} maps the
paraconsistent register onto the Boolean register $\mathbb{F}_2^m$ on which the algorithm
reads its expectation value, and Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan} expresses that value through
$\lambda_{\max}(A^{(m,\ell,d)})$, so the classical expectation value is the largest
eigenvalue of the paraconsistent token-word's ladder read through the coreflector.
\end{proof}

The matrix governing a state-of-the-art quantum algorithm is therefore the ladder of a
paraconsistent token-word read down to the classical register by the coreflector. The
algorithm's performance is a reading of the paraconsistent object through the lossy
retraction of Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy}.

\subsection{Direct computation of the classical image}

Both columns of the convergence table below come from one computation, not two sources.
The eigenvalue problem of Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan} is solved by shifted power iteration: a
shift makes the matrix positive semidefinite, so plain power iteration converges to the
true top eigenvalue rather than whichever spectral extreme has the larger magnitude,
which is the largest eigenvalue the lemma needs. The asymptotic form of
Proposition~\ref{prop:asymp} is computed in the same run from the same $p$ and $n$, so
the asymptotic column is output rather than a closed form substituted by hand. Alongside
both, the classical decoder itself, Prange's algorithm, is run for real: keep a random
subset of the constraints, interpolate the unique bounded-degree polynomial through one
point of each, measure its satisfaction against all constraints, and keep the best over
repeated draws. The finite eigenvalue and the asymptotic scalar and the decoder baseline
are all executed, every row.

\section{Empirical convergence}

Four balanced instances at a fixed ratio $n/p\approx0.495$ keep the asymptotic value of
Proposition~\ref{prop:asymp} nearly fixed across all four, while the finite matrix of
Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan} and its dimension $\ell+1$ grow.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{r r r r}
\toprule
$p$ & matrix size $(\ell+1)$ & $\langle s\rangle/p$ exact & asymptotic \\
\midrule
$101$   & $26\times26$     & $0.8938$ & $0.9316$ \\
$1009$  & $251\times251$   & $0.9229$ & $0.9317$ \\
$2003$  & $497\times497$   & $0.9259$ & $0.9316$ \\
$10007$ & $2478\times2478$ & $0.9296$ & $0.9316$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The exact value increases monotonically across the four scales, closing roughly a third
of the remaining gap to the asymptotic value with each doubling of $p$. The richer
operator-valued description at finite $m$ gives way to the poorer scalar limit as $m$
grows, the same direction the coreflector of Theorem~\ref{thm:two} moves, measured here
rather than assumed. By Theorem~\ref{thm:main} the mathematical content the finite
matrix computes was never reachable by anything other than a classical evaluation of the
paraconsistent original: there is no second, independently quantum object beside the
matrix, only the paraconsistent ladder and its classical image. The finite-size
convergence is the coarsening of Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy} made quantitative, the
operator-valued original standing above its scalar image and settling toward it as the
retraction is approached.

The practical ceiling on pushing the table to larger $p$ is not the eigenvalue solve,
which is linear in the ladder dimension and reaches the largest listed size in seconds
regardless of $m$, but the interpolation step of the classical decoder, cubic in the
polynomial degree. That is a limit on the baseline comparison, not on the quantity the
convergence is about, and it is the natural next point to lift with a shared-denominator
reformulation of the interpolation.

\section{Discussion}

One object has been traced through three descriptions. As a paraconsistent token-word it
is the split, fuse, and evaluate ladder on the $m$-fold register, an operator-valued
object that closes under the composition rule. As a classical image it is the
tridiagonal matrix $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$, the coreflector's reading of that ladder on the
Boolean register, governing the quantum algorithm's expectation value. As an asymptotic
limit it is the single scalar of Proposition~\ref{prop:asymp}, what the matrix becomes
when its structure is smoothed as $m\to p$. The three are one object at three levels of
coarsening, each arrow proved or measured: the coreflector from the paraconsistent to
the classical, the limit from the finite matrix to the scalar.

This is the program's thesis in one worked case. The quantum algorithm is the classical
image of a paraconsistent object, and the image is strictly poorer than the original in
a way that can be measured. The apparent quantum behavior, the concentration of
amplitude that gives the algorithm its advantage, is produced by the classical
evaluation of the paraconsistent ladder, with no additional physical ingredient. The
relationship is directional: the matrix is derived from the token-word, the scalar from
the matrix, and neither lower description asserts anything back up.

The tensor filtration of Section 5 is the mechanism throughout. The split and fuse are
the raising and lowering operators, the evaluate is the diagonal, and the whole
filtration is the tridiagonal ladder they build. Measurement is filtration through that
ladder, selecting a rung while the ladder persists, and the reversibility of the split
and fuse is why there is no collapse and no thermodynamic floor forcing dissipation. The
worked instance shows the same ladder governing a real algorithm, its largest eigenvalue
the algorithm's performance, its finite-to-asymptotic collapse the coreflector's
coarsening measured across four scales.

Look around you. The definite classical world of one-sided facts is the special
configuration that does not occur; a genuinely classical object would not hold together,
have color, or resist collapse, because its stability is the exclusion of paired
occupations, the value $B$ across every atom. What occurs is the ambient. The classical
image is what a retraction reads off it and the asymptotic scalar is what a limit reads
off the image, and at no level is the classical description the ground. It is a
coarsening, the coarsening is exactly lossy, and the loss is the state that carries
contradiction.

