input.new_string:
Both columns of the convergence table below come from one computation, not two sources.
Three procedures run every row.

The finite eigenvalue is the shifted power iteration. Build the $(\ell+1)\times(\ell+1)$
tridiagonal $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ of Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan}. Power iteration on $A$ converges
to the eigenvalue of largest magnitude, which need not be the largest eigenvalue when the
spectrum straddles zero. Shift by a bound on the spectral radius, iterating on
$A + \sigma I$ with $\sigma = \|A\|_\infty$ so that $A+\sigma I$ is positive semidefinite:

\begin{enumerate}
\item set $v$ to a random unit vector of length $\ell+1$;
\item repeat until the Rayleigh quotient settles: $w \leftarrow (A+\sigma I)v$, then
$v\leftarrow w/\|w\|$;
\item return $\lambda_{\max}(A) = v^{\top}(A+\sigma I)v - \sigma$.
\end{enumerate}

Each matrix-vector product is linear in $\ell$ because $A$ is tridiagonal, so the whole
solve is linear in the ladder dimension and reaches the $2478\times2478$ ladder in
seconds. Substituting $\lambda_{\max}$ into Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan} gives the exact
column.

The asymptotic scalar is computed in the same run from the same $p$ and $n$ by the closed
form of Proposition~\ref{prop:asymp}, so the asymptotic column is output rather than a
value copied in by hand.

The classical decoder baseline is Prange's algorithm, run for real:

\begin{enumerate}
\item draw a random size-$n$ subset $S$ of the constraints;
\item interpolate the unique degree-$<n$ polynomial $Q$ agreeing with one representative
of $F_y$ at each $y\in S$;
\item score $Q$ by its satisfaction count against all $m$ constraints;
\item repeat over many draws and keep the best score.
\end{enumerate}

The finite eigenvalue, the asymptotic scalar, and the decoder baseline are all executed
for every prime in the table.

